# 0.5 (`SEQUENCE-ID-COLLISION-ORDER`): fragment and operand ids collide with
# declarations written AFTER them, not only before. A section has one id
# namespace (core §1, sequence.md `lifeline` row), so the verdict cannot depend
# on line order.
#
# The engine's duplicate test did not consult fragment or operand ids, so only
# a fragment or operand declared after a clashing id was refused. `lifeline f`
# below was accepted, and an `in=f` written after it then resolved to the
# lifeline. Lines 21-22 are that case; lines 23-24 reuse each id on a later
# `message id=`, which the old engine also accepted unless a lifeline held it.
#
# Section 2 is the per-section half: the same two ids in a NEIGHBOURING section
# are a different namespace and produce no error.
figdown 0.5 sequence
title "Fragment and operand ids collide with later declarations"
lifeline c "Client"
lifeline s "Server"
fragment f "retry?" type=alt
operand o "first try" in=f
message c -> s "request" in=o
lifeline f "a lifeline reusing the fragment id"
lifeline o "a lifeline reusing the operand id"
message c -> s "reply" id=f
message c -> s "ack" id=o

figdown 0.5 sequence
lifeline f "the same id in a neighbouring section"
lifeline o "is a different namespace"
message f -> o "fine"
